    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1168">

                <pb facs="1180" n="1014" xml:id="pg1014"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.51/312</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Ambassador Fletcher </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-08-08T18:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 8, 1917, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>391. You may state to General Carranza that this Government will not veto loan
                    from the bankers of the United States to the Mexican Government. It would be
                    well, however, in discussing the matter with him to suggest as your opinion that
                    the chances of the Mexican Government obtaining such a loan from any bankers are
                    slight unless they are prepared to give some guaranty in regard to general
                    financial reforms and recognition of valid vested interests, and also that there
                    should be some distinct understanding that the money would be paid to Mexico in
                    instalments.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
